            <abstract>
                <p>According to <bibl corresp="http://zotero.org/groups/358366/items/ZABJTC9I"><seg type="journalArticle"><!-- attributes from original bibl--></seg><ptr target="bm:Triulzi2006Orality"/><title level="a">When orality turns to writing: two documents from Wälläga, Ethiopia</title><title level="j">Journal of African Cultural Studies</title><author><forename>Alessandro</forename><surname>Triulzi</surname></author><publisher>Routledge</publisher><date>2006</date><biblScope unit="volume">18</biblScope><biblScope unit="chapter">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43–55</biblScope><note type="accessed">2026-03-27</note><note type="url">https://www.jstor.org/stable/25473355?seq=1</note><note type="tags"><note type="tag">bm:Triulzi2006Orality</note></note><note type="tag">bm:Triulzi2006Orality</note><citedRange unit="page">50–51</citedRange></bibl>,
                    this text was produced by <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS4222Gabraeg"><roleName type="title">Daǧǧāč</roleName>
                    Gabra ʾƎgziʾabǝḥer alias Kumsaa Moroda</persName> <date notBefore="1920" notAfter="1923">in the early 1920s</date>
                    based on information of elders in order to legitimise his position as a leader of the Oromo in the region of 
                    <placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC4964Naqamt">Naqamtee</placeName>. The draft version was banned from being printed by 
                    <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS5226haylase">Ḫāyla Śǝllāse I</persName>. Later in <date when="1937">1937</date>, his son 
                    <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS4938HabtaMa">Hābta Māryām alias Hambissa Kumsaa</persName> updated the text by adding a short 
                    paragraph entitled <foreign xml:lang="am">Ya-Boranā ḥagaru Horr Walābu naw</foreign> ‘The country of the Boranā is 
                    Horr Walābu ...’. This version, which is witnessed in <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ANLcr48">Rome, Biblioteca dell’Accademia Nazionale dei Lincei, Conti Rossini 48</ref>, came to the hands of 
                    <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS7170Moreno">
                     Martino
                     Mario
                     Moreno 
               </persName> and <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS3182ContiRo">Carlo Conti Rossini</persName>, who produced two fragmentary 
                    translations in <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ANLcr48#accMat1">Rome, Biblioteca dell’Accademia Nazionale dei Lincei, Conti Rossini 48: p accMat1</ref> and <ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ANLcr48#accMat2">Rome, Biblioteca dell’Accademia Nazionale dei Lincei, Conti Rossini 48: p accMat2</ref>.</p>
